completion:
Folks have no right to censure other folks about their habits;
He who gave the squirrels bushy tails made the bobtails for the rabbits.
He who built the great big mountains hollowed out the little valleys,
He who made the streets and driveways wasn’t ashamed to make the alleys.

We are all constructed different, there aren’t two of us the same;
We can’t help our likes and dislikes, if we’re bad we aren’t to blame.
If we’re good, we needn’t show off, for you bet it isn’t our doing;
We get into certain channels that we just can’t help pursuing.

But we all fit into places that no other ones could fill,
And we do the things we have to, big or little, good or ill.
John can’t take the place of Henry, Sue and Sally aren’t alike;
Bass is nothing like a sucker, chub is nothing like a pike.

When you come to think about it, how it’s all planned out it’s splendid.
Nothing’s done or ever happens, without it’s something that’s intended;
No matter what you do, you have to, and it surely beats the dickens—
Viney, go put on the kettle, I’ve got one of master’s chickens.

I have got religion, honey, and I'm happy as a king;
Everything I see about me is just like sunshine in the spring;
And it seems like I don't want to do another blessed thing
But just run and tell the neighbors, and to shout and pray and sing.

I have shaken my fist at Satan, and I've given the world my back;
I don't want no hindering causes now a-bothering in my track;
For I'm on my way to glory, and I feel too sure to miss.
Why, there ain't no use in sinning when religion's sweet as this.

Talk about a man backsliding when he's on the gospel way;
No, sir, I have beat the devil, and Temptation's lost the day.
Going to keep my eyes right straight up, going to shut my ears, and see
What old project Mister Satan's going to try to work on me.

Listen, what's that sound I hear there? It's no one commence to sing;
It's a fiddle; get away there! Don't you hear that blessed thing?
Why, that's sweet as dripping honey, 'cause, you know, I draw the bow,
And when music's sure enough music, I'm the one that's sure to know.

Why, I've done the double shuffle, 'til a body couldn't rest,
Just a-hearing Sam the fiddler play that tune his level best;
I could cut a mighty caper, I could give a mighty fling
Just right now, I'm more than certain I could cut the pigeon wing.

Look here, what's this I've been saying? What on earth's took hold of me?
That old music came nigh running my religion up a tree!
Clear out with that there old fiddle, don't you try that trick again;
Didn't think I could be tempted, but you like to made me sin!

For the peace of my aching heels, set down;
    Don't fiddle that tune anymore.
  Don't you see how that melody stirs me up
    And begs me to take to the floor?
  You know I'm a Christian, good and strong;
    I worship from June to June;
  My prayers they are loud and my hymns are long:
    I beg you don't fiddle that tune.

  I'm a crick in my back and a misery here
    Where the joints are getting old and stiff,
  But it seems like you bring me the breath of my youth;
    Why, I'm certain I noticed a whiff.
  Don't fiddle that tune anymore, my child,
    Don't fiddle that tune anymore;
  I'll get up and take up this ground for a mile,
    And then I'll be churched for it, sure.

  Oh, fiddle that tune some more, I say,
    And fiddle it loud and fast:
  I'm a youngster again in the midst of my sin;
    The present's gone back to the past.
  I'll dance to that tune, so fiddle away;
    I know how the backslider feels;
  So fiddle it on until the break of the day
    For the sake of my aching heels.

prompt:
Rewrite the following poem in standard written English. Convert any non-standard spellings, grammar, and vocabulary into standard forms, while keeping the meaning, imagery, and emotional weight intact. Output only the rewritten poem.

--- SOURCE TEXT ---
De breeze is blowin' 'cross de bay.
      My lady, my lady;
  De ship hit teks me far away,
      My lady, my lady;
  Ole Mas' done sol' me down de stream;
  Dey tell me 't ain't so bad's hit seem,
      My lady, my lady.

  O' co'se I knows dat you'll be true,
      My lady, my lady;
  But den I do' know whut to do,
      My lady, my lady;
  I knowed some day we'd have to pa't,
  But den hit put' nigh breaks my hea't,
      My lady, my lady.

  De day is long, de night is black,
      My lady, my lady;
  I know you'll wait twell I come back,
      My lady, my lady;
  I'll stan' de ship, I'll stan' de chain,
  But I'll come back, my darlin' Jane,
      My lady, my lady.

  Jes' wait, jes' b'lieve in whut I say,
      My lady, my lady;
  D' ain't nothin' dat kin keep me 'way,
      My lady, my lady;
  A man's a man, an' love is love;
  God knows ouah hea'ts, my little dove;
  He'll he'p us f'om his th'one above,
      My lady, my lady.
--- END SOURCE TEXT ---

Keep the rewrite close to the original's length.

completion:
The breeze is blowing across the bay.
      My lady, my lady;
  The ship that takes me far away,
      My lady, my lady;
  Old Master has sold me down the stream;
  They tell me 'tis not as bad as it seems,
      My lady, my lady.

  Of course I know that you'll be true,
      My lady, my lady;
  But then I do not know what to do,
      My lady, my lady;
  I knew some day we'd have to part,
  But then it puts me nigh breaks my heart,
      My lady, my lady.

  The day is long, the night is black,
      My lady, my lady;
  I know you'll wait till I come back,
      My lady, my lady;
  I'll stand the ship, I'll stand the chain,
  But I'll come back, my darling Jane,
      My lady, my lady.

  Just wait, just believe in what I say,
      My lady, my lady;
  There ain't nothing that can keep me away,
      My lady, my lady;
  A man's a man, and love is love;
  God knows our hearts, my little dove;
  He'll help us from his throne above,
      My lady, my lady.

prompt:
Rewrite the following poem in standard written English. Convert any non-standard spellings, grammar, and vocabulary into standard forms, while keeping the meaning, imagery, and emotional weight intact. Output only the rewritten poem.

--- SOURCE TEXT ---
Dar's a lazy, sortah hazy
Feelin' grips me, thoo an' thoo;
An' I feels lak doin' less dan enythin';
Dough de saw is sharp an' greasy,
Dough de task et han' is easy,
An' de day am fair an' breezy,
Dar's a thief dat steals embition in de win'.

Kaint defy it, kaint deny it,
Kaze it jes won't be denied;
Its a mos' pursistin' stubbern sortah thin';
Anti Tox' doan neutrolize it;
Doctahs fail to analyze it;
So I yiel's (dough I despise it)
To dat res'less, wretchit fevah evah Sprin'.
--- END SOURCE TEXT ---

Keep the rewrite close to the original's length.

completion:
There's a lazy, sort of hazy
Feeling grips me, through and through;
And I feel like doing less than anything;
Though the saw is sharp and greasy,
Though the task at hand is easy,
And the day is fair and breezy,
There's a thief that steals ambition in the wind.

Can't defy it, can't deny it,
'Cause it just won't be denied;
It's a most persistent, stubborn sort of thing;
Anti-toxins don't neutralize it;
Doctors fail to analyze it;
So I yield (though I despise it)
To that restless, wretched fever every Spring.

prompt:
Rewrite the following poem in standard written English. Convert any non-standard spellings, grammar, and vocabulary into standard forms, while keeping the meaning, imagery, and emotional weight intact. Output only the rewritten poem.

--- SOURCE TEXT ---
Oh, I des received a letter f'om de sweetest little gal;
        Oh, my; oh, my.
  She's my lovely little sweetheart an' her name is Sal:
        Oh, my; oh, my.
  She writes me dat she loves me an' she loves me true,
  She wonders ef I'll tell huh dat I loves huh, too;
  An' my heaht's so full o' music dat I do' know what to do;
        Oh, my; oh, my.

  I got a man to read it an' he read it fine;
        Oh, my; oh, my.
  Dey ain' no use denying dat her love is mine;
        Oh, my; oh, my.
  But hyeah's de t'ing dat's puttin' me in such a awful plight,
  I t'ink of huh at mornin' an' I dream of huh at night;
  But how's I gwine to cou't huh w'en I do' know how to write?
        Oh, my; oh, my.

  My heaht is bubblin' ovah wid de t'ings I want to say;
        Oh, my; oh, my.
  An' dey's lots of folks to copy what I tell 'em fu' de pay;
        Oh, my; oh, my.
  But dey's t'ings dat I's a-t'inkin' dat is only fu' huh ears,
  An' I couldn't lu'n to write 'em ef I took a dozen years;
  So to go down daih an' tell huh is de only way, it 'pears;
        Oh, my; oh, my.
--- END SOURCE TEXT ---

Keep the rewrite close to the original's length.

completion:
Oh, I just received a letter from the sweetest little gal;
        Oh, my; oh, my.
She's my lovely little sweetheart and her name is Sal:
        Oh, my; oh, my.
She writes me that she loves me and she loves me true,
She wonders if I'll tell her that I love her, too;
And my heart's so full of music that I don't know what to do;
        Oh, my; oh, my.

I got a man to read it and he read it fine;
        Oh, my; oh, my.
There ain't no use denying that her love is mine;
        Oh, my; oh, my.
But here's the thing that's putting me in such an awful plight,
I think of her at morning and I dream of her at night;
But how am I going to court her when I don't know how to write?
        Oh, my; oh, my.

My heart is bubbling over with the things I want to say;
        Oh, my; oh, my.
And there's lots of folks to copy what I tell them for the pay;
        Oh, my; oh, my.
But there's things that I am thinking that are only for her ears,
And I couldn't learn to write them if I took a dozen years;
So to go down there and tell her is the only way, it appears;
        Oh, my; oh, my.
